  "summary": "Review writing in one story: Daniel's essay 'My year with Arabic', corrected by Mr Karim, with the spelling of hamza, ta marbuta and alif maqsura, joining letters, linking words and email phrases.",
  "intro": "Today is writing day. Daniel reads his مَقَالَة (essay) سَنَتِي مَعَ الْعَرَبِيَّة (My year with Arabic), and Mr Karim corrects it on the board, so every spelling point of the course comes back. Hamza sits on a seat chosen by the vowels around it: on a yaa with a kasra, as in قَارِئ (reader), on a waw with a damma, as in سُؤَال (question), otherwise on an alif, as in سَأَلَ (he asked). Ta marbuta ة has two dots: مَدْرَسَة (school) without them is misspelt. إِلَى (to) and عَلَى (on) end in alif maqsura ى, with no dots. Six letters never join to the next one: ا د ذ ر ز و. A good text is also organised: one idea in each فِقْرَة (paragraph), linking words like لٰكِنَّ (but), لِذٰلِكَ (so) and مَعَ أَنَّ (although), the letter phrases عَزِيزِي (dear) and أَرْجُو أَنْ تَكُونَ بِخَيْر (I hope you are well), and عَلَامَات التَّرْقِيم (punctuation). Write a مُسَوَّدَة (draft) first, then يُصَحِّحُ (he corrects) becomes your own job.",
