    "culture": "Arab schools take spelling seriously: pupils do إِمْلَاء (dictation) for years, and a teacher's red pen circles every missing dot and every hamza on the wrong seat. The two dots matter, because without them ة is read as a haa and ي as an alif maqsura. Handwriting is valued too: the art of الْخَطّ (calligraphy) is everywhere, from mosques to shop signs. Personal letters and postcards start warmly with عَزِيزِي or عَزِيزَتِي, and formal ones with تَحِيَّة طَيِّبَة وَبَعْدُ.",
